1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,870
It's February, somewhere in the northern forest.

2
00:00:02,870 --> 00:00:06,150
The snow is deeper than you are tall.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:09,430
And the food ran out eleven days ago.

4
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:11,787
You've boiled your moccasin leather.

5
00:00:11,787 --> 00:00:14,135
You've scraped lichen off the rocks.

6
00:00:14,135 --> 00:00:16,483
And tonight, for the first time,

7
00:00:16,483 --> 00:00:20,788
sitting by the fire, you look at the person next to

8
00:00:20,788 --> 00:00:24,310
you, and you notice a thought that frightens you.

9
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:28,385
The people who have lived in these forests for thousands of

10
00:00:28,385 --> 00:00:32,060
years have a name for what that thought can turn into.

11
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:33,975
Windigo. Wendigo. Witiko.

12
00:00:33,975 --> 00:00:38,520
A giant of hunger, made of frost and bone,

13
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:41,550
that eats people, and is never,

14
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:42,560
ever full.

15
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:47,519
And here's what makes it different from almost every other monster

16
00:00:47,519 --> 00:00:49,177
in the Ward Book.

17
00:00:49,177 --> 00:00:53,322
You don't only meet the Wendigo out in the woods.

18
00:00:53,322 --> 00:00:54,980
Sometimes, you become it.

19
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:57,867
This is the Ward Book.

20
00:00:57,867 --> 00:01:01,350
And tonight, how to survive the Wendigo.

21
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:04,319
First, whose story is this?

22
00:01:04,319 --> 00:01:09,457
The Wendigo belongs to Algonquian-speaking peoples of the northern forests.

23
00:01:09,457 --> 00:01:11,513
The Anishinaabe, or Ojibwe.

24
00:01:11,513 --> 00:01:13,568
The Cree. The Innu.

25
00:01:13,568 --> 00:01:19,220
And their neighbours around the Great Lakes and across the subarctic.

26
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:22,651
For them, this isn't a movie monster.

27
00:01:22,651 --> 00:01:25,682
It's a teaching, passed down for generations,

28
00:01:25,682 --> 00:01:27,846
and it's still told today.

29
00:01:27,846 --> 00:01:32,176
So tonight we're leaning on Indigenous writers and careful historians,

30
00:01:32,176 --> 00:01:35,640
and we're going to treat it that way.

31
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:41,235
The Anishinaabe writer Basil Johnston described the Weendigo as a giant.

32
00:01:41,235 --> 00:01:44,068
Gaunt to the point of starvation.

33
00:01:44,068 --> 00:01:48,791
Skin stretched over its bones, the grey colour of ash.

34
00:01:48,791 --> 00:01:50,680
Lips torn and bloody.

35
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:54,207
And here's the detail that matters most.

36
00:01:54,207 --> 00:01:57,335
Every time the Weendigo eats, it grows.

37
00:01:57,335 --> 00:02:00,015
So its hunger grows with it.

38
00:02:00,015 --> 00:02:02,249
It can never catch up.

39
00:02:02,249 --> 00:02:06,270
It's starving forever, no matter how much it eats.

40
00:02:06,670 --> 00:02:09,132
Johnston saw the lesson clearly.

41
00:02:09,132 --> 00:02:12,579
The Weendigo is what excess looks like.

42
00:02:12,579 --> 00:02:14,548
Greed, with a body.

43
00:02:14,548 --> 00:02:17,995
A hunger that destroys everything it touches,

44
00:02:17,995 --> 00:02:18,980
including itself.

45
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:24,648
To understand the Wendigo, you have to understand winter in the

46
00:02:24,648 --> 00:02:28,480
subarctic, before roads, before planes, before grocery stores.

47
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:31,955
In a bad winter, the animals vanish.

48
00:02:31,955 --> 00:02:35,908
Snow traps families in camps far from anyone else.

49
00:02:35,908 --> 00:02:40,740
Historians who study the fur trade era have found record after

50
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:45,572
record of hunger so severe that people starved to death in

51
00:02:45,572 --> 00:02:46,890
their own lodges.

52
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:49,692
And in that kind of hunger,

53
00:02:49,692 --> 00:02:53,295
there is one taboo that sits above every other.

54
00:02:53,295 --> 00:02:56,097
You do not eat another human being.

55
00:02:56,097 --> 00:02:58,899
Not ever. The Wendigo is that taboo,

56
00:02:58,899 --> 00:03:00,100
given a face.

57
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:04,060
Ward one. Share your food.

58
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:07,052
This is the deepest rule of all,

59
00:03:07,052 --> 00:03:09,274
and it's not about the monster.

60
00:03:09,274 --> 00:03:10,385
It's about you.

61
00:03:10,385 --> 00:03:12,977
The Wendigo is a spirit of greed.

62
00:03:12,977 --> 00:03:16,680
Of keeping more than you need while others go without.

63
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:20,470
In a world where survival depended on everyone,

64
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:24,707
hoarding food was the first step toward becoming something inhuman.

65
00:03:24,707 --> 00:03:28,097
So the first ward is the simplest one.

66
00:03:28,097 --> 00:03:30,640
When you have food, share it.

67
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:34,747
Ward two. Never cross the line.

68
00:03:34,747 --> 00:03:36,600
No matter what.

69
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,030
In many tellings, the surest way to become a Wendigo is

70
00:03:42,030 --> 00:03:43,860
to eat human flesh.

71
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:45,689
Even once. Even starving.

72
00:03:45,689 --> 00:03:48,890
Even if the person was already dead.

73
00:03:49,290 --> 00:03:52,575
It's a brutal rule, born in brutal winters.

74
00:03:52,575 --> 00:03:57,092
But it gave people something to hold on to when everything

75
00:03:57,092 --> 00:03:58,324
else had gone.

76
00:03:58,324 --> 00:04:02,430
Death, the stories said, is better than becoming the Wendigo.

77
00:04:02,830 --> 00:04:07,930
Ward three. Watch yourself, not just the woods.

78
00:04:08,330 --> 00:04:12,813
The scariest thing about the Wendigo is that the first signs

79
00:04:12,813 --> 00:04:14,850
are inside your own head.

80
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:19,333
In some accounts, the person turning feels sick at the sight

81
00:04:19,333 --> 00:04:20,555
of normal food.

82
00:04:20,555 --> 00:04:21,778
They grow cold.

83
00:04:21,778 --> 00:04:26,260
Distant. And they start to look at people the way a

84
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:27,890
hunter looks at game.

85
00:04:28,290 --> 00:04:31,028
So communities watched for these signs.

86
00:04:31,028 --> 00:04:33,765
And historians have recorded something heartbreaking.

87
00:04:33,765 --> 00:04:38,784
People who believed they were turning would sometimes ask their own

88
00:04:38,784 --> 00:04:42,890
families to kill them, before they could hurt anyone.

89
00:04:43,290 --> 00:04:44,457
Think about that.

90
00:04:44,457 --> 00:04:48,346
The ward isn't a charm you hang on your door.

91
00:04:48,346 --> 00:04:52,624
It's paying attention to yourself, and caring enough about the people

92
00:04:52,624 --> 00:04:54,180
around you to stop.

93
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:58,770
Ward four. Melt the heart of ice.

94
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:03,035
In many accounts, a Wendigo has a heart of ice.

95
00:05:03,035 --> 00:05:05,741
That's the frozen centre of the monster,

96
00:05:05,741 --> 00:05:08,060
and the key to beating it.

97
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:12,470
The stories say it can only truly be destroyed by fire,

98
00:05:12,470 --> 00:05:14,657
or by melting that frozen heart.

99
00:05:14,657 --> 00:05:16,844
Some tellings speak of hot fat,

100
00:05:16,844 --> 00:05:20,125
poured down the throat of someone who was turning,

101
00:05:20,125 --> 00:05:23,770
to melt the ice inside before it could take over.

102
00:05:24,170 --> 00:05:26,798
Whatever you make of the cure,

103
00:05:26,798 --> 00:05:28,549
the symbol is clear.

104
00:05:28,549 --> 00:05:31,177
Hunger and greed freeze the heart.

105
00:05:31,177 --> 00:05:34,680
Warmth, literal and human, is what thaws it.

106
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:40,430
Ward five. Trust the ones who know how to fight it.

107
00:05:40,830 --> 00:05:45,447
In the early nineteen hundreds, one of the most famous was

108
00:05:45,447 --> 00:05:48,805
Jack Fiddler, an Oji-Cree leader at Sandy Lake,

109
00:05:48,805 --> 00:05:50,903
in what's now northern Ontario.

110
00:05:50,903 --> 00:05:55,520
He was said to have defeated many Wendigos in his lifetime.

111
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:00,233
And that's where this legend runs straight into Canadian law.

112
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:04,978
In June nineteen oh seven, the North-West Mounted Police arrested Jack

113
00:06:04,978 --> 00:06:09,722
Fiddler and his brother Joseph, for killing a woman their community

114
00:06:09,722 --> 00:06:12,310
believed was turning into a Wendigo.

115
00:06:12,710 --> 00:06:17,214
Jack Fiddler took his own life in custody that September.

116
00:06:17,214 --> 00:06:22,169
Joseph was convicted, and died in custody in nineteen oh nine.

117
00:06:22,169 --> 00:06:24,872
To the police, it was murder.

118
00:06:24,872 --> 00:06:27,575
To their community, it was protection.

119
00:06:27,575 --> 00:06:32,530
Two worlds, two laws, and one terrible winter story caught between

120
00:06:32,530 --> 00:06:32,980
them.

121
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:37,192
There's another real case people always bring up.

122
00:06:37,192 --> 00:06:42,434
In eighteen seventy-nine, a Cree man known as Swift Runner was

123
00:06:42,434 --> 00:06:45,770
hanged at Fort Saskatchewan, in today's Alberta.

124
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:50,873
He'd killed and eaten members of his own family that winter,

125
00:06:50,873 --> 00:06:55,575
and he said a Wendigo spirit had taken hold of him.

126
00:06:55,575 --> 00:06:58,140
But the police noted something important.

127
00:06:58,140 --> 00:06:59,850
There was food nearby.

128
00:06:59,850 --> 00:07:01,560
This wasn't simple starvation.

129
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:07,864
Cases like this led twentieth-century psychiatrists to invent a diagnosis.

130
00:07:07,864 --> 00:07:13,178
Wendigo psychosis. A supposed madness, unique to northern peoples,

131
00:07:13,178 --> 00:07:16,720
where a person craves human flesh.

132
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:22,451
But in nineteen eighty-two, the anthropologist Lou Marano looked at the

133
00:07:22,451 --> 00:07:27,782
records closely, and argued that most so-called Wendigo cases were something

134
00:07:27,782 --> 00:07:29,720
else entirely. Not cannibals.

135
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:35,051
People accused of turning, who were killed by frightened communities before

136
00:07:35,051 --> 00:07:36,020
they could.

137
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:40,005
So the real horror of the Wendigo might not be a

138
00:07:40,005 --> 00:07:41,309
monster in the trees.

139
00:07:41,309 --> 00:07:44,895
It might be what fear and hunger do to a group

140
00:07:44,895 --> 00:07:48,480
of people, trapped together, in the longest night of the year.

141
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:49,965
Quick myth check.

142
00:07:49,965 --> 00:07:53,943
If you've seen a Wendigo in a game or a horror

143
00:07:53,943 --> 00:07:57,560
film, it probably had a deer skull and huge antlers.

144
00:07:57,960 --> 00:08:02,436
Look in the old accounts, and the antlers aren't there.

145
00:08:02,436 --> 00:08:06,464
Johnston's Weendigo is a starving giant with torn lips,

146
00:08:06,464 --> 00:08:07,806
not a deer.

147
00:08:07,806 --> 00:08:10,492
The antlers are a modern addition,

148
00:08:10,492 --> 00:08:13,177
from horror movies and video games.

149
00:08:13,177 --> 00:08:16,310
Spooky, sure. But it isn't the legend.

150
00:08:16,710 --> 00:08:19,199
So how do you survive the Wendigo?

151
00:08:19,199 --> 00:08:23,110
Strangely, this is the one legend where none of the wards

152
00:08:23,110 --> 00:08:25,599
are about running, or hiding, or salt,

153
00:08:25,599 --> 00:08:26,310
or iron.

154
00:08:26,710 --> 00:08:28,098
Share your food.

155
00:08:28,098 --> 00:08:29,949
Never cross the line.

156
00:08:29,949 --> 00:08:32,725
Watch yourself. Keep your heart warm.

157
00:08:32,725 --> 00:08:36,890
And listen to the people who know these woods.

158
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:41,207
The Wendigo is a warning about what happens when anyone,

159
00:08:41,207 --> 00:08:43,948
a person, a family, a whole society,

160
00:08:43,948 --> 00:08:47,473
takes more than it needs and still wants more.

161
00:08:47,473 --> 00:08:51,390
And that, honestly, is a monster that never went away.

162
00:08:51,790 --> 00:08:55,692
Next week, we leave the frozen forest for a darker kind

163
00:08:55,692 --> 00:08:58,885
of wood. A hut that stands on chicken legs.

164
00:08:58,885 --> 00:09:00,659
A fence made of bones.

165
00:09:00,659 --> 00:09:03,497
And an old woman who might help you,

166
00:09:03,497 --> 00:09:05,980
or might put you in the oven.

167
00:09:06,380 --> 00:09:08,217
It's Baba Yaga.

168
00:09:08,217 --> 00:09:14,955
And she has very particular rules about the questions you're allowed

169
00:09:14,955 --> 00:09:21,692
to ask. Subscribe, so the Ward Book is open when she

170
00:09:21,692 --> 00:09:25,980
comes. And until then, share your dinner.
